s is O(N^2) total. We can use OpenMP SIMD pragmas on independent loops: the horizontal sliding window has a sequential dependency in sum variable but we can compute prefix sums? Actually horizontal max over fixed colSum array: we need to find max of sum of s consecutive elements. That's also a sliding window with O(n). It is inherently sequential if using running sum, but we could use prefix sums and then vectorize the max reduction across j (independent reads) - that might be faster? Let's think.\n\nGiven colSum[j] for fixed i, define pref[0]=0; pref[k+1]=pref[k]+colSum[k]. Then submatrix sum starting at column j is pref[j+s]-pref[j]. We need max over j=0..n-s. This can be computed with a vectorized loop: for each j compute val = pref[j+s]-pref[j], then reduce max. The prefix computation itself is sequential but O(n) and cheap; the max reduction can use SIMD. Alternatively, we could maintain running sum horizontally as in baseline, which has dependency chain of length n-s per row (add/subtract), but that's also fine. Which is faster? Probably prefix + vectorized max might be better because it avoids sequential add/sub dependencies and allows compiler auto-vectorization with #pragma omp simd reduction(max:mx). But we need to ensure correctness.\n\nWe can combine vertical update loop (colSum[j] += ...) which is independent across j, so SIMD-friendly. Horizontal prefix also has dependency but only one per row; the max loop over j is independent and vectorizable with reduction.\n\nLet's design code:\n\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cassert>\n#include <algorithm> // for std::max? already used, but include maybe not needed\n#ifdef _OPENMP\n#include <omp.h>\n#endif\n\nint main() {\n    int n,s;\n    if (scanf(\"%d %d\", &n, &s) != 2) return 0; // robust input\n    std::vector<int> matrix(n*n);\n    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++) \n            scanf(\"%d\", &matrix[i*n+j]);\n\n    assert(s<n && n<8000 && s>0 && n>0);\n\n    // column sums for first window\n    std::vector<int> colSum(n, 0);\n    for(int i=0;i<s;i++)\n        #pragma omp simd\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++)\n            colSum[j] += matrix[i*n+j];\n\n    int mxSum = INT_MIN; // need <climits>\n    std::vector<int> pref(n+1);\n\n    for(int i=0; i+s<=n; ++i) {\n        // compute prefix sums of colSum\n        pref[0]=0;\n        #pragma omp simd? No, sequential dependency.\n        for(int j=0;j<n;j++) pref[j+1] = pref[j]+colSum[j];\n        int rowMax = INT_MIN;\n        #pragma omp simd reduction(max:rowMax)\n        for(int j=0; j+s<=n; ++j) {\n            int val = pref[j+s]-pref[j];\n            if(val>rowMax) rowMax=val;\n        }\n        if(rowMax > mxSum) mxSum = rowMax;\n\n        // update colSum for next i, unless last\n        if(i+s < n) {\n            #pragma omp simd\n            for(int j=0;j<n;j++)\n                colSum[j] += matrix[(i+s)*n+j] - matrix[i*n+j];\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", mxSum);\n}\n\nBut we need to include <climits> or use std::numeric_limits. Also note that the prefix loop is sequential; compiler may not vectorize due to dependency, but it's O(n) and cheap relative to n^2? Actually total work: for each i (n-s+1 ~ n), we do prefix O(n) + max O(n) + update O(n) => 3*n*(n-s+1) ~ 3N^2. Baseline is S*N^2, so if S large this is much better; if S small maybe similar? But prior experiments show ~0.012s vs baseline 0.133s, so good.\n\nWe can optimize further: Instead of recomputing prefix from scratch each row, we could maintain horizontal running sum directly as in sliding window, but that has sequential dependency per column step. The prefix approach allows vectorized max reduction. However, the prefix loop itself is a serial scan; maybe we can use SIMD for update and max only.\n\nAlternative: Use two-pass vertical sums with precomputed row-wise? But current should be fine.\n\nWe need to ensure no integer overflow: sum fits int32 per problem guarantee, but intermed